description:
crate-jmap-chat-server/src/memory.rs:2800-2828 (new code introduced this pass via commit 8853c54) contains four sequential blocks of the shape:

  if let Some(e) = exceeded("roles", cur_roles, add_roles, limits.max_roles_per_space) {
      return Some(e);
  }
  if let Some(e) = exceeded("members", cur_members, add_members, limits.max_space_members) {
      return Some(e);
  }
  if let Some(e) = exceeded("channels", cur_channels, add_channels, limits.max_channels_per_space) {
      return Some(e);
  }
  if let Some(e) = exceeded("categories", cur_categories, add_categories, limits.max_categories_per_space) {
      return Some(e);
  }
  None

The shape encodes 'try four checks in order, return the first hit'. Rust's iterator combinator for this is .find_map:

  [
      ("roles", cur_roles, add_roles, limits.max_roles_per_space),
      ("members", cur_members, add_members, limits.max_space_members),
      ("channels", cur_channels, add_channels, limits.max_channels_per_space),
      ("categories", cur_categories, add_categories, limits.max_categories_per_space),
  ]
  .into_iter()
  .find_map(|(label, cur, add, cap)| exceeded(label, cur, add, cap))

Engineering basis: the function's contract — 'first cap to trip determines the SetError' — is encoded structurally in the sequence rather than narratively. A future reviewer reading the four if-let-Some blocks must verify that all four return on Some(e) and that the trailing None matches the contract. The combinator form expresses the contract in one line.

Counter-argument (taste-of-bikeshed risk): the if-let chain is more debuggable under a step-debugger than a combinator chain, and a future addition of a fifth cap (e.g. invites-per-Space) requires the same effort either way. Held at P3 because the engineering basis is real but the impact is local to one function's readability — not soundness, not correctness, not API surface.

New-code finding: introduced this review pass via 8853c54 (apply_space_patch backend-canonical cap enforcement).
